Add bounded function-level parallel decompiler

Add decompiler skip-counter optimizations (v9) and intra-function parallel options

Skip-counter infrastructure on Funcdata: globalModCount (aggregate),
irModCount (IR structure only), vnCreateCount (new varnodes), typeModCount
(datatype mutations).  Counters are bumped at the IR mutation points in
funcdata_op.cc and funcdata_varnode.cc (opSetOpcode/opSetOutput/opSetInput/
opSwapInput, newConstant/newUnique/newVarnodeOut/newUniqueOut).

ActionPool gains a rule_modcount_skip Action flag (action.hh/action.cc) so
individual actions may opt out of redundant re-runs when the relevant counter
has not changed since the last successful apply().  ActionBlockStructure
opts in (blockaction.hh).

ActionInferTypes (coreaction.cc/hh) caches lastSeenIrMod and
lastTypesConverged across passes: when the previous writeBack reported
no datatype change AND irModCount is unchanged, the entire apply() is a
no-op.  writeBack now calls bumpTypeModCount() instead of
bumpGlobalModCount() so nzm-only consumers can discriminate.

Heritage (heritage.cc/hh) skips placeMultiequals() and rename() when the
disjoint TaskList is empty after the per-space loop -- a common case
in late passes where every reachable space is already in SSA form.

Datatests: 668/668 pass.

Also adds Java plumbing for intra-function parallel decompile options
(DecompileOptions.java: parallelDecompile, workers, minOps;
ElementId.java: ELEM_PARALLELDECOMPILE) and a benchmark
(ParallelIntraFunctionDecompileBenchmark.java).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

Two-phase parallel ActionPool infrastructure (experimental, default OFF)

Adds the foundation for intra-function parallel decompilation via a two-phase
ActionPool design.  The infrastructure is OFF by default — when the env vars
DECOMP_INTRA_WORKERS and DECOMP_INTRA_MINOPS are unset, ActionPool::apply
takes the existing v9 serial path verbatim and output is byte-identical to v9.

Components:
- parallel.{hh,cc}: persistent thread pool (lazy singleton, condition-variable
  task queue, ~85 lines).  Linked via -pthread in Makefile.
- Rule::canApply(op, data) virtual on the Rule base class: optional pure-read
  precondition predicate that returns -1 (unknown), 0 (certain not to fire), or
  1 (may fire).  Rules opt in via the has_canapply flag bit so phase 1 can
  skip the virtual call for rules that don't override.
- canApply implementations for three high-volume rules: RuleEarlyRemoval,
  RuleCollapseConstants, RulePropagateCopy.
- ActionPool::applyParallel(): phase 1 parallel canApply across N workers
  builds a per-op skip mask; phase 2 walks the snapshot serially, dispatching
  rules but bypassing those whose canApply returned 0.
- PcodeOpBank::size() and Funcdata::obSize() accessors used for the minOps gate.

Activation: setenv DECOMP_INTRA_WORKERS=N (N>=2) and DECOMP_INTRA_MINOPS=M to
engage the parallel path for functions with at least M ops.

Current status (negative result, documented for future work):
- Correctness: 668/668 datatests pass with workers=4 and 8 — semantically
  equivalent decompilation.
- SHA-identity: NOT preserved with workers>=2.  Phase 1 fingerprints state S0;
  phase 2 mutations evolve state; skip decisions based on S0 may miss firings
  that would happen at later state, leading to different intra-pass cascade
  order and thus different late-stage variable naming.  Output is semantically
  identical (variables represent the same values, types, control flow) but
  textually differs (uVar15 vs uVar16, etc.).
- Performance: parallel path is 1-5% SLOWER than serial on the top30 libc
  benchmark, because per-call thread sync overhead exceeds the small phase 1
  work parallelized (3 rules' precondition checks per op).

Future work needed for positive speedup:
- Massive canApply coverage (~20+ hot rules) to grow phase 1 work
- Per-rule TEST/APPLY split so phase 2 can run rule bodies in parallel with
  fine-grained Varnode-level locks (or speculative + retry)
- Block-DAG static partitioning so independent basic blocks process in parallel
  without per-op locking

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

Add engineering plan + Path 1 first-pass audit for intra-function parallel decompile

Plan document (INTRA_FUNCTION_PARALLEL_PLAN.md, 635 lines) covers all three tracks needed
for positive intra-function speedup:

  - Path 1: per-rule TEST/APPLY split for all 152 Rule subclasses (~6.5 eng-weeks)
  - Path 2: block-DAG static partitioning with conflict graph + greedy coloring (~3.3 weeks)
  - Path 3: per-Varnode/op shared_mutex for parallel doApply (~4.2 weeks)

Total realistic calendar with one mid-senior engineer FT: 4-5 months end-to-end.
Two engineers in parallel: ~9 weeks.

The plan explicitly excludes shortcuts: SHA-identity vs serial is dropped (semantic
equivalence via 668 datatests + cross-binary AST-equivalence is sufficient), but the
implementation is otherwise required to be production-grade.

Companion audit document (INTRA_FUNCTION_PARALLEL_RULE_AUDIT.md, 160 lines): first-pass
automated categorization of all 152 rules via audit_rules.py:

  - A  : 85 rules — straightforward split (mutation only at apply path)
  - B  : 42 rules — loop + interleaved multi-mutation
  - C  :  3 rules — known side effects in fail path (need resolution strategy)
  - A_NOOP : 22 rules — delegate to external helpers (require manual review since
             regex audit can't see into SplitVarnode::applyRuleIn etc.)

audit_rules.py is checked in for reproducibility — re-runnable as the codebase evolves.

This is the design + audit deliverable; implementation work is the next phase.

