P4-d5: conditional-lock elision recovers serial baseline

The Path 4 locks (Funcdata::poolMutex / unionMutex / Varnode::descendMutex)
showed +6.9% serial overhead vs stock master on libcrypto heavy corpus in
the apples-to-apples bench (14d3dd95b2).  Root cause: each lock_guard
acquire is ~5-50 ns of LOCK CMPXCHG even when uncontended, and the hot
mutators (opSetInput/Output, addDescend, newConstant) are called millions
of times per function.

Resolution: gate every project-internal mutex through a single
process-global atomic<bool> g_parallelActive set once at startup from the
environment.  When parallel mode is off, the new RAII wrappers
(ConditionalRecursiveLock / ConditionalMutexLock) compile down to a
single relaxed load + predictable branch — effectively eliminating the
lock cost on the serial path.

Init points: consolemain (decomp_opt/dbg), ghidra_process (Ghidra-driven),
and test (datatests).  Flag stays false unless DECOMP_INTRA_WORKERS>1 or
DECOMP_PARALLEL_GUARDCALLS=1 is set in the environment.

Touched 34 lock sites across funcdata.cc, funcdata_op.cc,
funcdata_varnode.cc, varnode.cc.  No behavioral change in parallel mode
(flag true → identical to before).  Stats locks in action.cc unchanged
(already env-gated by getRuleStatsEnabled()).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

P4-d5b: gate atomic counter fetch_add behind isParallelActive

After P4-d5, ours P=1 was still +5.4% vs stock (was +6.9% pre-d5).
Profile shows the remaining gap is mostly LOCK XADD on the mod-counter
atomics (globalModCount, irModCount, vnCreateCount, typeModCount),
called several times per opSetInput / opSetOutput / newConstant.

LOCK XADD costs ~10-30ns even uncontended (single-core).  Plain
store(load+1) on x86-64 with relaxed memory order compiles to three
MOV/INC instructions — no bus lock.

Since these counters are monotonically non-decreasing skip-decision
signals (not balance/quota counters), the only correctness requirement
is "any mutation must lift the counter at least once."  That holds in
serial mode trivially with non-atomic increment; only parallel mode
needs fetch_add to avoid lost updates from concurrent worker threads.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

P4-d5c: move per-Varnode descendMutex to a shared 256-entry pool

Each Varnode held a 40-byte std::mutex member.  On a corpus with 100k+
varnodes per function, that grew sizeof(Varnode) from ~144B to ~184B —
bumping each Varnode from 2 to 3 cache lines.  Cache footprint on the
hot Varnode-accessor paths (canApply scans, opSetInput descend lookups)
suffered roughly +50%.

The mutex is now drawn from a 256-entry process-global pool indexed by
hash(varnode_addr >> 6).  False sharing across pool entries is ~1/256;
only matters in parallel mode (which isParallelActive() gates).  Net:

  - sizeof(Varnode) back to pre-P4 baseline
  - addDescend / eraseDescend get one hash op (uintptr_t shift + mask)
  - serial path: ConditionalMutexLock skips the lock entirely (no change)
  - parallel path: same semantics, equivalent contention

